\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{The physical spectral Hamiltonian}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the [physical transfer operator](fdip-0083), let
#{\lambda_0=\|T_{\mathrm{phys}}\|>0} and fix a time step
#{a_t>0}. There is an orthonormal eigenbasis #{(\psi_j)} with
#{0<\lambda_j\le\lambda_0}, including an eigenvector at
#{\lambda_0}. Define
##{\widehat T=\frac{T_{\mathrm{phys}}}{\lambda_0},\qquad
 E_j=\frac1{a_t}\log\frac{\lambda_0}{\lambda_j}.}
The [transfer Hamiltonian](fdip-007W) is
#{H=-a_t^{-1}\log\widehat T}, with the explicit domain and action
##{\begin{aligned}
D(H)&=\left\{f=\sum_jc_j\psi_j:
                    \sum_jE_j^2|c_j|^2<\infty\right\},\\
Hf&=\sum_jE_jc_j\psi_j.
\end{aligned}}
It is nonnegative and self-adjoint, and
#{e^{-a_tH}=\widehat T}. If the physical Hilbert space is infinite
dimensional, #{H} is unbounded. This assertion does not specify
the multiplicity of its zero eigenspace. Here physical names the
gauge-invariant subspace of \ref{fdip-0083}. The operator #{H} is
the Euclidean generator on that subspace. A real-time continuation
changes the real form, and the Lorentzian algebra stays a different
real form.}

\proof{
\p{The spectral theorem for compact self-adjoint operators applies
to the positive trace-class operator of \ref{fdip-0083}.
Injectivity removes its zero eigenspace, so the positive eigenvectors
form a complete orthonormal basis. Its nonzero norm is an eigenvalue.
In the finite-dimensional case the list is finite; otherwise its
eigenvalues tend to zero.}

\p{The eigenspace-density assertion is formalized by
\leanref{ContinuousLinearMap.orthogonalComplement_iSup_eigenspaces_eq_bot}:
for a compact symmetric operator on a complete real or complex
inner-product space, the span of all eigenspaces has zero orthogonal
complement. This reference supplies that spectral premise; the
logarithm, its domain and its core are addressed below.}

\p{Under the unitary coefficient map #{f\mapsto(c_j)}, the
normalized transfer is multiplication by #{\lambda_j/\lambda_0}.
Its spectral logarithm is therefore multiplication by the real
nonnegative sequence #{E_j} on the stated maximal domain.
This diagonal operator is self-adjoint; finite coefficient
truncations form a core. Exponentiating on the basis proves the
transfer identity. If the list is infinite, #{\lambda_j\to0}
implies #{E_j\to\infty}, proving unboundedness.}
}

\p{This is the same normalization and domain construction as the
[Gaussian oscillator](fdip-007W), without its explicit eigenvalues.
It supplies a Hamiltonian at finite spatial volume. A unique vacuum,
limits in time or volume, and a continuum field theory are separate
questions, not conclusions of this spectral construction.}
